UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 12, Lesson 2: Planning a healthy meal', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Planning a healthy meal means choosing foods from several groups and thinking about cost, time, allergies and what the family enjoys. Steps: decide the meal, list the foods by group, see what you already have, write a shopping list and note safe preparation steps. Adults help with sharp tools and hot items, and anyone with an allergy or medical need follows their own advice.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same idea to a new game or situation, then compare two choices for safety and inclusion.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nA family has a small shopping budget and one person with a nut allergy. Plan a protein food for the meal that avoids nuts.\n\nFEEDBACK\nOptions: eggs, chicken, baked beans or fish. Always read labels for hidden nuts and follow medical advice. A good plan solves the problems (budget and allergy) while keeping variety.\n\nWATCH FOR\nThinking a healthy meal is always expensive or hard to make. Simple foods such as beans, eggs, seasonal vegetables and oats can be affordable and healthy.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nPlan one dinner for your household that includes at least four food groups. Write the menu by group, a shopping list, two safety steps for preparation and one way to adjust for anyone''s food needs or allergies. Cook only with an adult.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one choice you changed and why. Any movement is optional, adult-supervised and can be done in a gentler way or described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"PDHPE","learning_intention":"Planning a healthy meal means choosing foods from several groups and thinking about cost, time, allergies and what the family enjoys. Steps: decide the meal, list the foods by group, see what you already have, write a shopping list and note safe preparation steps. Adults help with sharp tools and hot items, and anyone with an allergy or medical need follows their own advice.","lesson_number":2,"lesson_role":"Practise and apply","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Clear, safe movement space with an adult nearby for any physical activity","Optional: soft ball, cushion or water bottle"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["PH2-IHW-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Plan one dinner for your household that includes at least four food groups. Write the menu by group, a shopping list, two safety steps for preparation and one way to adjust for anyone''s food needs or allergies. Cook only Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original response on new material: Options: eggs, chicken, baked beans or fish. Always read labels for hidden nuts and follow medical advice. A good plan solves the problems (budget and allergy) while keeping variety. Reasoning: I used a meal plan by attending to a shopping list rather than copying the worked model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Thinking a healthy meal is always expensive or hard to make. Simple foods such as beans, eggs, seasonal vegetables and oats can be affordable and healthy. Correction: Options: eggs, chicken, baked beans or fish. Always read labels for hidden nuts and follow medical advice. A good plan solves the problems (budget and… Why: the correction restores accurate use of a meal plan.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Thinking a healthy meal is always expensive or hard to make. Simple foods such as beans, eggs, seasonal vegetables and oats can be affordable and healthy. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating a meal plan on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with a meal plan, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail. / Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Options: eggs, chicken, baked beans or fish. Always read labels for hidden nuts and follow medical advice. A good plan solves the problems (budget and… This matches the lesson skill for a meal plan.","options":["Copy the example and call that independent work.","Options: eggs, chicken, baked beans or fish. Always read labels for hidden nuts and follow medical advice. A good plan solves the problems (budget and…","Thinking a healthy meal is always expensive or hard to make. Simple foods such as beans, eggs, seasonal vegetables and oats can be affordable and healthy.","Ignore the safety rule because the first idea looked exciting."],"question":"A family has a small shopping budget and one person with a nut allergy. Plan a protein food for the meal that avoids nuts. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Thinking a healthy meal is always expensive or hard to make. Simple foods such as beans, eggs, seasonal vegetables and oats can be affordable and healthy. That misconception weakens accuracy; the other choices align with the lesson idea.","options":["Thinking a healthy meal is always expensive or hard to make. Simple foods such as beans, eggs, seasonal vegetables and oats can be affordable and healthy.","Planning a healthy meal means choosing foods from several groups and thinking about cost, time, allergies and what the family enjoys. Steps: decide the meal,…","Options: eggs, chicken, baked beans or fish. Always read labels for hidden nuts and follow medical advice. A good plan solves the problems (budget and…","Careful use of a shopping list with clear evidence."],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when practising a meal plan?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and named a shopping list, not by copying the model.","options":["Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence.","Choose randomly because any option could be right.","Treat a skipped permission or safety step as if it did not matter.","Apply the idea with new evidence and name the a shopping list used."],"question":"A learner is practising a meal plan. Which approach best shows understanding?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Options: eggs, chicken, baked beans or fish. Always read labels for hidden nuts and follow medical advice. A good plan solves the problems (budget and… uses safe, healthy, respectful choices appropriately for this lesson.","options":["Ignore the safety rule because the first idea looked exciting.","Copy the example and call that independent work.","Options: eggs, chicken, baked beans or fish. Always read labels for hidden nuts and follow medical advice. A good plan solves the problems (budget and…","Thinking a healthy meal is always expensive or hard to make. Simple foods such as beans, eggs, seasonal vegetables and oats can be affordable and healthy."],"question":"Which response best shows careful thinking about a meal plan?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows.","options":["Copy a classmate’s sentence with no local detail.","Thinking a healthy meal is always expensive or hard to make. Simple foods such as beans, eggs, seasonal vegetables and oats can be affordable and healthy.","Name a specific source, cue or example and say what it shows about a meal plan.","Make a bigger claim without naming any source or cue."],"question":"Which habit strengthens evidence about a shopping list?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea with concrete detail.","options":["Treat a skipped permission or safety step as if it did not matter.","Ignore the safety rule because the first idea looked exciting.","Example plan for a build-your-own wrap dinner for four. Grain: wholemeal wraps. Protein: shredded chicken and beans. Vegetables: lettuce,…","Thinking a healthy meal is always expensive or hard to make. Simple foods such as beans, eggs, seasonal vegetables and oats can be affordable and healthy."],"question":"Using this lesson idea — Planning a healthy meal means choosing foods from several groups and thinking about cost, time, allergies… — which choice is best?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Respect and accuracy mean checking evidence and honouring limits/protocols.","options":["Treat every online claim as equally reliable.","Check facts/cues, acknowledge limits, and avoid inventing details.","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete.","Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster."],"question":"Which action shows respect and accuracy together?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the skill.","options":["Plan one dinner for your household that includes at least four food groups. Write the menu by group, a shopping list, two safety steps for…","Retype the worked example with no new example.","Copy the example and call that independent work.","Thinking a healthy meal is always expensive or hard to make. Simple foods such as beans, eggs, seasonal vegetables and oats can be affordable and healthy."],"question":"Which task best shows independent use of a meal plan?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating a meal plan on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Original response on new material: Options: eggs, chicken, baked beans or fish. Always read labels for hidden nuts and follow medical advice. A good plan solves the problems (budget and allergy) while keeping variety. Reasoning: I used a meal plan by attending to a shopping list rather than copying the worked model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason.","question":"Name one likely error with a meal plan, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Common error: Thinking a healthy meal is always expensive or hard to make. Simple foods such as beans, eggs, seasonal vegetables and oats can be affordable and healthy. Correction: Options: eggs, chicken, baked beans or fish. Always read labels for hidden nuts and follow medical advice. A good plan solves the problems (budget and… Why: the correction restores accurate use of a meal plan.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested. Share only what you feel comfortable sharing.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-pdhpe-y3-w12-l2","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA PDHPE alignment using catalogue outcome codes (PH2-*); content-point mapping remains pending human review. Physical activities are optional, adult-supervised and adaptable; no child is required to share personal details.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise a meal plan.","Read the mission before you start moving or deciding.","Keep your own safety and everyone''s inclusion in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not keep everyone safe and included."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the cue or rule that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore safety, consent or inclusion.","Prefer a reasoned, respectful choice over one that merely looks fun."],"submit":["Confirm that your reasons and reflection are both present.","Proofread names, steps and safety words.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact cue, rule or choice before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key cues.","Say what each cue does for safety, health or friendship.","A final label alone is weaker than a stated reason."],"teach2":["Compare an unsafe or unkind choice with a better one.","Change the choice or strategy, not only the words.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh place, game or situation that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original reasons and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill in a new situation."]},"subject":"Healthy, Safe and Active Lifestyles","success_criteria":["I can use a meal plan with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify a shopping list and explain why it matters.","I can practise allergies and needs independently on a new prompt.","I can notice safe preparation needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Planning a healthy meal means choosing foods from several groups and thinking about cost, time, allergies and what the family enjoys. Steps: decide the meal, list the foods by group, see what you already have, write a shopping list and note safe preparation steps. Adults help…","options":["A guess with no reason.","Planning a healthy meal means choosing foods from several groups and thinking about cost, time, allergies and what the family enjoys. Steps: decide the meal, list the foods by group, see what you already have, write a shopping list and note safe preparation steps. Adults help with sharp tools and hot items, and anyone with an allergy or medical need follows their own advice.","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect.","An answer copied without understanding."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about a meal plan?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample plan for a build-your-own wrap dinner for four. Grain: wholemeal wraps. Protein: shredded chicken and beans. Vegetables: lettuce, tomato and grated carrot. Milk group: grated cheese. Fruit: sliced mango afterwards. Shopping list: wraps, lettuce. Safety: an adult cooks the chicken and uses the knife; children wash the vegetables.\n\nTRY FIRST\nA family has a small shopping budget and one person with a nut allergy. Plan a protein food for the meal that avoids nuts.\n\nFEEDBACK\nOptions: eggs, chicken, baked beans or fish. Always read labels for hidden nuts and follow medical advice. A good plan solves the problems (budget and allergy) while keeping variety.","explain":"Planning a healthy meal means choosing foods from several groups and thinking about cost, time, allergies and what the family enjoys. Steps: decide the meal, list the foods by group, see what you already have, write a shopping list and note safe preparation steps. Adults help with sharp tools and hot items, and anyone with an allergy or medical need follows their own advice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Thinking a healthy meal is always expensive or hard to make. Simple foods such as beans, eggs, seasonal vegetables and oats can be affordable and healthy. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"From menu to shopping list.","caption":"List by group.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">List</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">foods by group</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Shop</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">what we need to buy</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which cue or rule matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the cues, rules or choices that keep everyone safe and included before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this example: Example plan for a build-your-own wrap dinner for four. Grain: wholemeal wraps. Protein: shredded chicken and beans. Veg Then write your own reasons.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which cue or rule matters most.\nJuno: Name the cues, rules or choices that keep everyone safe and included before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this example: Example plan for a build-your-own wrap dinner for four. Grain: wholemeal wraps. Protein: shredded chicken and beans. Veg Then write your own reasons."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others. Supported choices follow the actual cues, rules and purpose of the task.","options":["A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no reason.","Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about a meal plan?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample plan for a build-your-own wrap dinner for four. Grain: wholemeal wraps. Protein: shredded chicken and beans. Vegetables: lettuce, tomato and grated carrot. Milk group: grated cheese. Fruit: sliced mango afterwards. Shopping list: wraps, lettuce. Safety: an adult cooks the chicken and uses the knife; children wash the vegetables. Look at each cue, rule or choice that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key cues, rules or choices from the example and say what each one does for safety, health or relationships.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful reasoning names the cue, rule or strategy and the reason it helps, not only a label such as \"good\".","explain":"Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final label without a reason does not show PDHPE thinking. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Show the reasoning","visual":[{"alt":"Allergy and cooking safety.","caption":"Safety comes first.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Allergy</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">read labels</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Safety</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">adult uses the knife</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison. Name the specific PDHPE error, then show the safer or more respectful choice. Supported choices follow the actual cues, rules and purpose of the task.","options":["A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no reason.","Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison. Name the specific PDHPE error, then show the safer or more respectful choice."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about a meal plan?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nThinking a healthy meal is always expensive or hard to make. Simple foods such as beans, eggs, seasonal vegetables and oats can be affordable and healthy. Here is a more helpful example: Example plan for a build-your-own wrap dinner for four. Grain: wholemeal wraps. Protein: shredded chicken and beans. Vegetables: lettuce, tomato and grated carrot. Milk group: grated cheese. Fruit: sliced mango afterwards. Shopping list: wraps, lettuce. Safety: an adult cooks the chicken and uses the knife; children wash the vegetables.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against choice does not work, then write a safer, kinder or healthier choice with a reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nOptions: eggs, chicken, baked beans or fish. Always read labels for hidden nuts and follow medical advice. A good plan solves the problems (budget and allergy) while keeping variety.","explain":"Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison. Name the specific PDHPE error, then show the safer or more respectful choice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the wording without changing the choice does not fix the error. Thinking a healthy meal is always expensive or hard to make. Simple foods such as beans, eggs, seasonal vegetables and oats can be affordable and healthy. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example. Review safety, fairness and inclusion. Supported choices follow the actual cues, rules and purpose of the task.","options":["An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no reason.","Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example. Review safety, fairness and inclusion.","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about a meal plan?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nPlanning a healthy meal means choosing foods from several groups and thinking about cost, time, allergies and what the family enjoys. Steps: decide the meal, list the foods by group, see what you already have, write a shopping list and note safe preparation steps. Adults help with sharp tools and hot items, and anyone with an allergy or medical need follows their own advice. Now choose a different situation to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe or practise one fresh situation that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new situation should use the same PDHPE idea as the example, but happen in a different place or with different people.","explain":"Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example. Review safety, fairness and inclusion.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to a new situation. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Transfer to a new situation"}],"title":"Year 3, Week 12, Lesson 2: Planning a healthy meal","unit_number":3,"unit_title":"Healthy food and nutrition","week":12,"week_in_unit":2,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nExample plan for a build-your-own wrap dinner for four. Grain: wholemeal wraps. Protein: shredded chicken and beans. Vegetables: lettuce, tomato and grated carrot. Milk group: grated cheese. Fruit: sliced mango afterwards. Shopping list: wraps, lettuce. Safety: an adult cooks the chicken and uses the knife; children wash the vegetables.\n\nWrite what makes this example safe, healthy or respectful, naming the cues, rules or choices used.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"From menu to shopping list.","caption":"List by group.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">List</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">foods by group</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Shop</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">what we need to buy</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Allergy and cooking safety.","caption":"Safety 